<span class="cat-badge">Guide &middot; Local Areas</span>
<h1>Long Bien by Motorbike: Motorbike Day-Ride Ideas in Hanoi</h1>
<p>A day ride is the moment a rental stops being transport and starts being the point, and Long Bien is the best home base for one in Hanoi. The district holds the rental counter itself at 112 Nguyen Van Cu, sits on the east bank of the Red River with the riverside roads and the historic bridge in its own streets, and stands at the city end of the roads that lead out eastward and northward. That combination means the ride can stay local, cross the river into the centre, or leave town entirely, and every version starts from home turf. What follows is a menu of day rides built around landmarks and rider comfort, deliberately flexible, because the best day ride turns around when the rider says so, not when a schedule does.</p>
<p>We start with rides that never leave the district, add the classic crossing into central Hanoi, sketch the lakeside loop to the north, describe the countryside ride out of the eastern exits, and then cover the practical layer: which machine suits which day, what to carry, how weather should edit the plan, and how to sequence a stay so the days build on each other. No journey times are quoted anywhere, because Hanoi's roads do not negotiate with timetables. Start at the <a href="/motorbike-rental-hanoi-old-quarter/">Old Quarter motorbike rental</a> hub, see the machines at the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/">Hanoi motorbike rental</a> guide, and browse wider riding advice in the <a href="/motorbike-guides/">motorbike guides</a>.</p>
<nav class="toc">
<ol>
<li><a href="#sec-1">The riverside home ride</a></li>
<li><a href="#sec-2">The classic crossing into central Hanoi</a></li>
<li><a href="#sec-3">The lakeside loop north</a></li>
